StellarMasses
2021-06-04
Edward Taylor <entaylor@swin.edu.au>

This DMU provides stellar masses, restframe photometry, and other
ancillary stellar population parameters from stellar population fits
to multiband SEDs for all objects with D > 0 across the five GAMA
survey regions. Multiple tables are provided, corresponding to
different sources of photometry, as detailed below. Note that these
tables include many stars and galaxies with unknown redshifts (NQ=1)
for which the derived values are not meaningful. Users need to
exercise their own quality control.

______________________________________________________________________

The following tables are provided by this DMU:

Table	      	     	 Source photometry (DMU/table)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
StellarMassesGKV	 <a href="/dr4/schema/table.php?id=682">gkvInputCatv02/gkvInputCatv02</a>
StellarMassesLambdar	 <a href="/dr4/schema/table.php?id=519">LambdarPhotometryv01/LambdarCatv01</a>
StellarMassesPanChrom	 <a href="/dr4/schema/table.php?id=505">PanchromaticPhotomv03/21BandPhotomv03</a>
StellarMassesG02CFHTLS	 <a href="/dr4/schema/table.php?id=581">G02InputCatv07/G02CFHTLSInputCatv04</a>
StellarMassesG02SDSS	 <a href="/dr4/schema/table.php?id=580">G02InputCatv07/G02SDSSInputCatv01</a>

In each case, the photometry table was matched with <a href="/dr4/schema/table.php?id=488">DistancesFramesv14</a>
and all objects with Z_TONRY > 0 were selected for the corresponding
StellarMasses table, regardless of the value of NQ.

When using the tables of this DMU, please refer to:
<a href="https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2011MNRAS.418.1587T">E.N. Taylor et al., 2011, MNRAS, 418, 1587</a>.

Compared to previous versions of this DMU, the process of deriving its
results has been completely refactored, including some small bug
fixes. There are several minor changes to the modelling compared to
what is described in the original paper: 1.) the fits are made to a
fixed restframe wavelength range (3000 - 11000 A), rather than a fixed
set of bands; 2.) slight changes to the priors, which are uniform in:
age, log(tau), log(Z), and EBV.

NOTE: The values in these tables are based on aperture (Petro, AUTO,
LAMBDAR, or ProFound) photometry, which may miss a significant
fraction of a galaxy's light. In past versions of this DMU, the tables
contained a 'fluxscale' parameter, which could be used to rescale the
aperture-derived values to match the total flux as inferred from
Sersic modelling. Since v24, this parameter is no longer included in
the various tables.

______________________________________________________________________

DMU dependencies:
    DistancesFramesv14     - Heliocentric and flow-corrected redshifts
    GalacticExtinctionv03  - Foreground dust attenuation corrections
    21BandPhotomv03        - Multiband photometry for G09/12/15
    LambdarCatv01          - Multiband photometry for G09/12/15
    G02TilingCatv07        - Catalogue cross-identifications for G02
    G02SDSSInputCatv01     - SDSS photometry for G02 targets
    G02CFHTLSInputCatv04   - CFHTLS photometry for G02 targets
    gkvInputCat02          - Multiband photometry for G09/12/15/23
______________________________________________________________________


STELLAR POPULATION SYNTHESIS MODELLING: ______________________________

The data provided by these tables have been derived through stellar
population synthesis modelling of broadband photometry. In the fits,
each photometric point is weighted so that the fits are effectively to
the fixed restframe wavelength range 3000 to 11000 Angstrom for all
galaxies at all redshifts. The modelling is done using the Bruzual &
Charlot (2003) stellar evolution models, assuming a Chabrier (2003)
stellar initial mass function (IMF) and the Calzetti et al. (2000)
dust curve. The SPS models used in the fitting are defined via four
parameters: time since formation (i.e. age), e-folding time for the
(exponentially declining) star formation history, stellar metallicity,
and dust attenuation (Section 3.1 of Taylor et al. 2011). The values
of all derived parameters given in the tables, including the formal
uncertainties, have been derived in a Bayesian way (Sections 3.2-3.4
of Taylor et al. 2011).

DISTANCES AND COSMOLOGY: _____________________________________________

All values are derived for the concordance (Omega_m, Omega_L, h) =
(0.3, 0.7, 0.7) cosmology. Stellar population parameters including
ages, dust extinctions, and mass-to-light ratios are cosmology
independent, inasmuch as they pertain to the stellar populations at
the time of observation. Restframe luminosities and thus the total
stellar mass do depend on distance, and so on h^-2. These can be
scaled in the usual way, such that a higher value of h implies a lower
distance, and thus a lower luminosity/mass (see below). Note that
distances have been computed using the flow-corrected redshifts
'Z_TONRY' given in DistancesFramesv14. These values have been derived
using the Tonry et al. (2000) flow model for very low redshifts, and
then tapering to a CMB-centric frame for z > 0.03, as described by
<a href="https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2012MNRAS.421..621B">Baldry et al. (2012)</a>.

BASED ON (FINITE) APERTURE PHOTOMETRY: _______________________________

The results given in these tables have been derived from aperture
photometry. The various DMUs that produced the different photometric
catalogues used here, employed a variety of schemes to define the
apertures, which all aim to get consistent *colours* across the
SED. These different schemes all aim to capture most of the galaxy
flux, in order to get a representative colour for each galaxy as a
whole (i.e. to limit the impact of colour gradients). But because all
of these schemes use a finite aperture, they are all prone to miss
some low-level flux beyond the aperture.

In general, therefore, an aperture correction is required for
integrated quantities (i.e. stellar mass or luminosity) to account for
mass/flux that falls beyond the finite aperture used for the SEDs. In
previous versions of this DMU, the column 'fluxscale' had been
provided for this purpose. Since v24, this quantity is no longer
included in the catalogues.

If missed flux/aperture corrections are a concern, it is now the
responsibility of the user to derive an appropriate aperture
correction as:

    fluxscale = 10**-0.4 * (m_total - m_aperture)

where m_total and m_aperture represent the total and aperture flux in
a given band or bands. A good choice for total magnitude estimates are
profile-fit magnitudes like those given in the <a href="/dr4/schema/dmu.php?name=SersicPhotometry">SersicPhotometry</a> or
<a href="/dr4/schema/dmu.php?name=BDDecomp">BDDecomp</a> DMUs. The rescalings for mass and absolute magnitudes are
then:

    logmstar_total = logmstar + log10(fluxscale)
or:
    absmag_total = absmag - 2.5 * log10(fluxscale)

Note that the numerical value of fluxscale should be greater than or
equal to 1.

GOODNESS OF FIT STATISTIC: ___________________________________________

From v16, this DMU includes the Posterior Predictive P-value (PPP) as
a goodness of fit statistic, which can be roughly thought of as a
Bayesian analogue to the frequentist reduced chi^2. The PPP is a
useful tool for checking whether or to what extent the model is
consistent with the data. Given the observed data and the posterior
PDF on the properties of the model, the PPP quantifies the fraction of
future observations that would be predicted to be as discrepant as the
observed data. As an example, a PPP < 0.01 could be taken to imply
that the current data invalidate even the 'most likely' model at 99%
confidence.

Whether and how best to use this information is probably application
specific, and is left to the user to decide. In general terms, a PPP~0
generally indicates a likely catastrophic failure of the SED fit, but
can also indicate, for example, just one bad photometric point.
Individual SED fits with PPP < 0.2 should be treated with some
suspicion. A PPP of ~0.5 implies a perfect fit. A PPP of ~1 implies an
improbably good fit - either the observational uncertainties have been
overestimated, or the data are being over-fit.

DIFFERENT MASS ESTIMATORS: ___________________________________________

There are three different masses given in the tables:

  * logmstar -- this is the total mass of all luminous material plus
      remnants, but excluding mass recycled back into the ISM, at the
      time of observation.
      
  * logmremnants -- this is the total mass of all non- or sub-luminous
      stellar remnants (e.g. white dwarfs), at the time of
      observation.
      
  * logmintsfh -- this is the total mass of all stars formed, obtained
      by integrating over the implied SFH, up to the time of
      observation.

Note that the difference between the combined mass of stars and
remnants on the one hand, and the integral of the SFH on the other, is
mass lost in stellar winds or other ejecta. In this sense, embedded in
the values of logmintsfh is the assumption that none of this 'lost'
mass is 'recycled' back into new generations of stars.

It is envisaged that most users will want to use the 'logmstar's,
which are the most directly comparable to what is most often used in
the literature. To get just the luminous stars, one should subtract
'mremnants' from 'mstar'. Finally, the integral of the implied SFH
gives the total mass of all stars formed, from the earliest times up
to the epoch of observation. In most cases, 'logmintsfh' is the most
directly comparable to the stellar masses that are output by
simulations.

RESTFRAME PHOTOMETRY AND COLOURS: ____________________________________

All of the photometry provided by these tables has been inferred from
the SPS fits as an attribute of the SP models. All restframe
photometry uses AB zeropoints and is k-corrected to redshift 0, with
no evolution corrections. The table contains restframe (g - i) and
(u - r) colours (in the columns 'gminusi' and 'uminusi', respectively)
as well as restframe photometry in individual bands (e.g. 'absmag_g'
and 'absmag_i'). Because in the fitting process, ugriz fluxes are
highly correlated with one another, the formal uncertainties on
'gminusi' or 'uminusr' cannot in general be derived from the
'delrfphot'. It is thus preferable to use, e.g., 'gminusi' and the
associated uncertainty 'delgminusi' rather than 'absmag_g' -
'absmag_i'.

Further, note that the fact that the fits are constrained to the
restframe wavelength range 3000-11000 A means that any restframe
photometry blueward of the u band and redward of the Y band should be
considered as extrapolations of the fit-to data.

INTRINSIC VERSUS EFFECTIVE RESTFRAME PHOTOMETRY: _____________________

The quantities 'absmag_X' should be understood as *effective*
restframe absolute magnitudes in the X-band; that is, the absolute
magnitudes corresponding to the light the observer actually sees from
the galaxy, without correction for extinction within the galaxy
itself. In contrast, the quantities 'absmag_X_stars' give *intrinsic*
absolute magnitudes in the X-band, which should be understood to be
the total light emitted by stars; that is, corrected for internal dust
extinction according to 'dustEBV'. In both cases, these values are
derived from the SPS fits as properties of the stellar populations in
a similar way to ages, metallicities, mass-to-light ratios,
etc. (i.e. a la Equation 5 of Taylor et al. 2011).

